 One more part hiding in your robot: the <Term id="microcontroller">microcontroller</Term> — a tiny cheap computer whose job is to open and close many little circuits very fast. When your program says *turn LED on*, the microcontroller closes the LED's loop. Your blocks have been operating switches all along.
 
+## Under the hood: the one equation
+
+You now know voltage pushes and current flows. The third piece is <Term id="resistance">resistance</Term> — how hard the path fights back, measured in ohms (Ω). All three lock together in <Term id="ohms-law">Ohm's law</Term>:
+
+```
+current = voltage ÷ resistance        I = V ÷ R
+```
+
+That is the whole thing. Try it: a 9V battery through a 470Ω resistor gives 9 ÷ 470 = 0.019 A, or about **19 mA**. Push harder (more volts) and more flows. Fight harder (more ohms) and less flows.
+
+**Why you should care right now:** an LED will happily draw far more current than it can survive and burn itself out in about a second. So you put a resistor in series to hold the current down — that is what the little resistor next to every LED in every kit is doing. It is not decoration; it is Ohm's law keeping your LED alive.
+
+A quick way to sanity-check any circuit you build later:
+
+| If you… | Then current… | Because |
+|---|---|---|
+| swap 9V for 3V | drops to a third | less push, same fight |
+| swap 470Ω for 220Ω | roughly doubles | less fight, same push |
+| forget the resistor | shoots up | almost nothing fighting it — 💥 |
+
+
 **Want to see it for real?** The resources page links SparkFun's "What is a Circuit?" — the classic illustrated walk-through, with a simulation of current flowing.
 
